<!--
  Sizing for a 768 MiB cgroup. Same lesson as ADR-004 #8: a container memory
  limit does not size the process, it only kills it. ClickHouse reads cgroup
  limits on modern versions, but the absolute cap below is stated anyway so the
  number is visible next to the mem_limit it has to stay under, rather than
  derived from a ratio applied to whatever RAM detection happens to return.

  512 MiB of server cap against a 768 MiB limit leaves ~256 MiB of headroom for
  the things the cap does not count: page cache, the librdkafka consumer's own
  buffers, and the jemalloc arenas.

  mark_cache_size is the trap here. Its default is 5 GiB (not an allocation, but
  ClickHouse budgets against it), which on a small container crowds out
  everything else. See ADR-005 #9.

  Note for anyone editing this file: XML comments cannot contain a double
  hyphen, so the em dashes used everywhere else in this repo are spelled as
  parentheses or colons here. A stray one is a hard startup failure, not a
  warning: "Failed to merge config with ... SAXParseException: Invalid token".
-->
<clickhouse>
    <max_server_memory_usage>536870912</max_server_memory_usage>
    <max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>0.7</max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>

    <mark_cache_size>134217728</mark_cache_size>
    <index_mark_cache_size>33554432</index_mark_cache_size>
    <!-- The whole hot table is ~50K rows and lives in the OS page cache; a
         second decompressed copy inside ClickHouse buys nothing here. -->
    <uncompressed_cache_size>0</uncompressed_cache_size>
    <compiled_expression_cache_size>0</compiled_expression_cache_size>
    <mmap_cache_size>256</mmap_cache_size>

    <max_concurrent_queries>8</max_concurrent_queries>

    <!--
      background_pool_size is deliberately NOT set here, and the reason is worth
      recording because shrinking it is the standard low memory advice.

      It does not shrink alone. ClickHouse sanity checks at boot that several
      merge_tree thresholds sit below
      background_pool_size * background_merges_mutations_concurrency_ratio, and
      it exits 36 (BAD_ARGUMENTS) rather than starting degraded. Cutting the
      pool to 4 tripped number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_mutation
      (default 20, needs < 8); raising it to 8 then tripped
      number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_optimize_entire_partition
      (default 25, needs < 16). Each fix revealed the next.

      Chasing that to the end would mean three magic numbers in this file to buy
      a handful of lazily committed thread stacks, on a server whose actual
      memory goes to caches and query working sets, both of which ARE capped
      above. The pool stays at its default. Same shape as ADR-004 #8: these are
      a system of constraints, not independent dials. See ADR-005 #9.

      background_schedule_pool_size is left alone for a second reason: the Kafka
      engine's consumer runs on it.
    -->
</clickhouse>
